      "answer": "Primary diagnosis: Septic shock secondary to acute complicated urinary tract infection (pyelonephritis or prostatitis) with urinary retention and acute kidney injury (AKI). Immediate management priorities: 1) Fluid resuscitation: initial 30 mL/kg crystalloid (e.g., 2 L lactated Ringer's) over first 3 hours, reassess using dynamic measures (e.g., passive leg raise, pulse pressure variation) to avoid fluid overload. 2) Vasopressor initiation: start norepinephrine if MAP remains <65 mmHg after initial fluid bolus, titrate to MAP ≥65 mmHg. Consider adding vasopressin if norepinephrine dose >0.5 mcg/kg/min. 3) Source control: Foley catheter already placed; perform digital rectal exam to assess for prostatic abscess; obtain renal ultrasound to rule out obstruction or abscess. If abscess found, arrange drainage (urology consult). Empiric antimicrobial therapy: Given recent antibiotic use (UTI treated orally), severity, and high procalcitonin, start broad-spectrum agents covering ESBL and Pseudomonas: e.g., piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV q6h (adjusted for CrCl ~20 mL/min: 4.5 g q8h) or meropenem 1 g IV q8h (preferred if high ESBL risk). Add vancomycin 15-20 mg/kg IV loading dose (target trough 15-20) for MRSA coverage until cultures negative. Duration: 10-14 days for complicated UTI, longer if prostatitis (4-6 weeks). ICU admission: immediate transfer to ICU for hemodynamic monitoring (arterial line, central line if needed), continuous vital sign monitoring, and titration of vasopressors. Hemodynamic monitoring: consider echocardiography to assess volume status and cardiac function. Renal replacement therapy: not immediately indicated; monitor urine output, creatinine, and potassium. If persistent oliguria (<0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours) or severe acidosis (pH <7.15) or hyperkalemia (>6.5), start continuous RRT. Adjunctive therapies: 1) Corticosteroids: consider IV hydrocortisone 200 mg/day if vasopressor-dependent after adequate fluid resuscitation (e.g., norepinephrine >0.25 mcg/kg/min for >4 hours). 2) Blood product thresholds: transfuse platelets only if <10,000/mcL or active bleeding (not present); no evidence for RBC transfusion unless Hb <7 g/dL. 3) Lactate clearance goal: target lactate reduction by ≥20% within 6 hours; repeat lactate q2-4h. Disposition planning: ICU admission, step-down to ward after vasopressor wean and clinical improvement. Potential complications: acute respiratory distress syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, acute kidney injury requiring RRT, multi-organ failure, antibiotic-associated Clostridioides difficile infection. Need for vasopressors: yes, norepinephrine first-line. Assessment for ICU admission: mandatory. Prostate examination: digital rectal exam to assess for prostatitis/abscess. Imaging considerations: renal ultrasound (or CT if stable) to evaluate for obstruction, abscess, or emphysematous pyelonephritis. Source control beyond catheterization: if prostatic abscess, urology for drainage; if renal abscess, percutaneous drainage.",
